1
00:00:04,675 --> 00:00:10,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you tell how you broke into baseball, where and when, in the minor leagues?

2
00:00:10,377 --> 00:00:12,837
[SPEAKER_00]: You mean through, I mean not Semapro?

3
00:00:13,437 --> 00:00:15,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, even Semapro.

4
00:00:15,138 --> 00:00:17,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe they come over here to look me over.

5
00:00:17,458 --> 00:00:19,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Tell me just where you grew up and how.

6
00:00:19,199 --> 00:00:21,219
[SPEAKER_00]: I grew up in City South Bend right here.

7
00:00:21,239 --> 00:00:22,620
[SPEAKER_00]: I was born on Cherry Street over there.

8
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:23,860
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm raised on Chestnut over here.

9
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:29,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, after I married and played ball, I lived in different places, but my folks lived here since 1916, see.

10
00:00:31,352 --> 00:00:33,612
[SPEAKER_00]: And then after they died, I inherited the home.

11
00:00:33,652 --> 00:00:35,233
[SPEAKER_00]: That was my oldest brother that you saw now.

12
00:00:35,273 --> 00:00:36,133
[SPEAKER_00]: He's 77.

13
00:00:37,553 --> 00:00:38,533
[SPEAKER_00]: And we inherited the home.

14
00:00:38,553 --> 00:00:41,314
[SPEAKER_00]: So we're here ever since 19, when I quit playing ball in 43.

15
00:00:42,694 --> 00:00:44,814
[SPEAKER_00]: So there's no place like home, you know.

16
00:00:44,834 --> 00:00:48,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, the neighborhood kind of deteriorated a little bit, but I mean, other than that.

17
00:00:48,955 --> 00:00:51,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Where did your parents move here?

18
00:00:51,215 --> 00:00:52,656
[SPEAKER_00]: To America?

19
00:00:53,036 --> 00:00:54,436
[SPEAKER_00]: They came here in 1892, but they met here.

20
00:00:54,456 --> 00:00:56,656
[SPEAKER_00]: They married in 1895, and my oldest brother was born in 1897.

21
00:01:00,888 --> 00:01:01,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Right there on Cherry Street.

22
00:01:02,028 --> 00:01:02,849
[SPEAKER_00]: You were born in 1907?

23
00:01:03,049 --> 00:01:04,669
[SPEAKER_00]: I was born in 1907, yeah.

24
00:01:04,709 --> 00:01:05,669
[SPEAKER_00]: I was born ten years later.

25
00:01:05,689 --> 00:01:08,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, where did they come from in Poland?

26
00:01:08,750 --> 00:01:11,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, they come from Poland, yeah.

27
00:01:12,091 --> 00:01:13,392
[SPEAKER_00]: I have to think of the town.

28
00:01:13,452 --> 00:01:15,472
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't recall the name of the little village just now.

29
00:01:15,973 --> 00:01:18,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Although Mother, she was a maid, but from the German side.

30
00:01:18,854 --> 00:01:21,434
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, Poland was divided between half Russian and half Polish.

31
00:01:22,195 --> 00:01:24,135
[SPEAKER_00]: And they were from the German side of Poland.

32
00:01:25,316 --> 00:01:29,897
[SPEAKER_00]: And she was like a housemaid, I guess, in Berlin apartments, you know.

33
00:01:30,690 --> 00:01:32,210
[SPEAKER_00]: or rich people had them for apartments.

34
00:01:32,610 --> 00:01:35,131
[SPEAKER_00]: Daddy, he skipped just before Kaiser got into the Army.

35
00:01:36,051 --> 00:01:38,612
[SPEAKER_00]: So he was 18, I guess, or 19 when he skipped.

36
00:01:39,272 --> 00:01:42,853
[SPEAKER_00]: But he had a brother here in Milwaukee already that probably brought him in, you know.

37
00:01:44,053 --> 00:01:51,474
[SPEAKER_00]: And just before Hitler, I mean, just before Kaiser was going to get him into service, which was forced then, you know, they just got him working.

38
00:01:51,594 --> 00:01:54,735
[SPEAKER_00]: He took off over here because his brother was over here.

39
00:01:55,775 --> 00:01:58,097
[SPEAKER_00]: I got the name of the little town now.

40
00:01:58,117 --> 00:02:00,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Slupce, yeah, the name of the town was Slupce.

41
00:02:00,178 --> 00:02:00,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Slupce?

42
00:02:00,778 --> 00:02:01,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Slupce, yeah.

43
00:02:02,019 --> 00:02:06,202
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's in the eastern, well, the eastern part of Poland, I mean the western part of Poland.

44
00:02:06,923 --> 00:02:09,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you speak Polish at all?

45
00:02:09,384 --> 00:02:11,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh yes I do, sure.

46
00:02:11,646 --> 00:02:13,607
[SPEAKER_00]: I went to Polish school for eight years.

47
00:02:13,947 --> 00:02:16,689
[SPEAKER_00]: I had Polish catechism, Polish history, Polish religion.

48
00:02:17,550 --> 00:02:19,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Speak it and write it.

49
00:02:19,171 --> 00:02:23,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh yes sir, St. Henry's Parish right over here, about 15 minute walk from here.

50
00:02:23,914 --> 00:02:25,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Still belong to the same parish.

51
00:02:28,957 --> 00:02:47,621
[SPEAKER_00]: After the war, I had some of my cousins write, that's after the First World War, write over here, you know, I was about 12, 13 years old, but this must be the 20s, and they wrote in German, you know, and nobody could bother, forgot the German, you know, bother, they could probably speak it, but they couldn't read it or write it.

52
00:02:48,561 --> 00:02:55,462
[SPEAKER_00]: So I went and got it interpreted, you know, and I wrote them back in Polish, you know, and they wrote back and says, how can somebody born in this country

53
00:02:56,410 --> 00:03:00,411
[SPEAKER_00]: learned to write so well in Polish, and you know, I said, well, I learned it in school.

54
00:03:00,711 --> 00:03:02,772
[SPEAKER_00]: I learned my ABCs in English and Polish more.

55
00:03:04,133 --> 00:03:05,573
[SPEAKER_00]: So I can speak both languages.

56
00:03:05,953 --> 00:03:13,816
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a few words that you can't, you don't use too often, you probably can't recall, you know, because it's like somebody asked me the other day, how do you say plumber in Polish?

57
00:03:13,836 --> 00:03:17,817
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, if he's in Poland, there was no plumbers in Poland, you know.

58
00:03:18,757 --> 00:03:25,019
[SPEAKER_00]: So we looked it up, the Polish book, and it's like a, like a Polish, English version of it, but spelled different, you know.

59
00:03:25,687 --> 00:03:27,328
[SPEAKER_00]: And then Olownik is one of them, see?

60
00:03:27,908 --> 00:03:31,289
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't use that kind of language around here, you know, because you don't have the use for it.

61
00:03:32,189 --> 00:03:33,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you ever get back to Poland?

62
00:03:33,750 --> 00:03:34,710
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I never went to Poland.

63
00:03:34,750 --> 00:03:35,430
[SPEAKER_00]: I was born here.

64
00:03:35,510 --> 00:03:36,191
[SPEAKER_00]: I never went back.

65
00:03:36,211 --> 00:03:37,211
[SPEAKER_00]: I never had no desire.

66
00:03:37,471 --> 00:03:40,232
[SPEAKER_00]: I asked my daddy why he never went back to Poland.

67
00:03:40,252 --> 00:03:45,113
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, dad, if I, son, he says, the reason I left Poland, because there was nothing there for me.

68
00:03:45,133 --> 00:03:47,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Just that they were like sharecroppers, more or less, you know?

69
00:03:47,994 --> 00:03:49,415
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, there was no future for me there.

70
00:03:49,455 --> 00:03:50,075
[SPEAKER_00]: So I took off.

71
00:03:50,095 --> 00:03:52,136
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, I just never had no desire to go back.

72
00:03:52,616 --> 00:03:53,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Under the conditions they had then.

73
00:03:53,896 --> 00:03:55,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, maybe a little better now.

74
00:03:56,335 --> 00:03:57,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Those days they didn't have nothing.

75
00:03:57,676 --> 00:04:02,480
[SPEAKER_00]: They had the lords, you know, the landlords, or what do you want to call them, sharecroppers, all they were.

76
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:06,183
[SPEAKER_00]: And they just got a pig and a cow and just made a living, that's all.

77
00:04:08,205 --> 00:04:09,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you play baseball?

78
00:04:09,286 --> 00:04:10,827
[SPEAKER_00]: What did your baseball skills teach you?

79
00:04:10,867 --> 00:04:12,608
[SPEAKER_00]: Right over here, right over here in the field.

80
00:04:12,628 --> 00:04:13,569
[SPEAKER_00]: We didn't have nothing else to do.

81
00:04:13,589 --> 00:04:14,510
[SPEAKER_00]: We didn't have no bicycles.

82
00:04:14,530 --> 00:04:15,651
[SPEAKER_00]: We didn't have no roller skates.

83
00:04:15,671 --> 00:04:16,331
[SPEAKER_00]: We didn't have nothing.

84
00:04:16,371 --> 00:04:16,972
[SPEAKER_00]: Couldn't afford it.

85
00:04:17,112 --> 00:04:19,254
[SPEAKER_00]: So there's a lot of open fields over here.

86
00:04:19,274 --> 00:04:22,296
[SPEAKER_00]: And we played ball in the morning and the afternoons and evenings.

87
00:04:23,457 --> 00:04:24,298
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all there was to do.

88
00:04:25,628 --> 00:04:26,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing else to do.

89
00:04:27,188 --> 00:04:28,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Naughty kids, what'd they do?

90
00:04:28,149 --> 00:04:30,149
[SPEAKER_00]: They got an automobile, they got bicycles.

91
00:04:30,189 --> 00:04:31,490
[SPEAKER_00]: And trouble.

92
00:04:31,850 --> 00:04:33,230
[SPEAKER_00]: And they'd get in trouble, that's true.

93
00:04:34,770 --> 00:04:36,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's all we had to do was play.

94
00:04:37,631 --> 00:04:42,732
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, some of them developed more than others, but I mean, at least each neighborhood had a team, more or less.

95
00:04:43,933 --> 00:04:45,413
[SPEAKER_00]: One gang would play the other, that's all.

96
00:04:45,913 --> 00:04:48,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have an early desire to make the big leagues?

97
00:04:48,494 --> 00:04:51,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, after I got the summer Pro Bowl, yes, I did.

98
00:04:51,255 --> 00:04:52,695
[SPEAKER_00]: When did this happen?

99
00:04:53,072 --> 00:04:55,513
[SPEAKER_00]: This was when I was about 15, 16 years old.

100
00:04:55,573 --> 00:04:56,474
[SPEAKER_00]: I was 16 years old.

101
00:04:56,734 --> 00:04:58,234
[SPEAKER_00]: I just went through the eighth grade.

102
00:04:58,254 --> 00:05:02,836
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't go to high school because the need at home, you know, for money at home.

103
00:05:02,956 --> 00:05:05,497
[SPEAKER_00]: After all, there was eight of us children.

104
00:05:05,557 --> 00:05:08,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Daddy only working, there were two brothers working, they were married.

105
00:05:09,399 --> 00:05:11,240
[SPEAKER_00]: So when I was 16 years old, I started working.

106
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:13,321
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I was playing ball for student leaguers.

107
00:05:15,042 --> 00:05:20,664
[SPEAKER_00]: And we played a lot of semi-pro then, because all around South Bend, town was 30, 40, 50 miles away.

108
00:05:21,504 --> 00:05:22,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Every one of them had a team, you know.

109
00:05:23,410 --> 00:05:25,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Now they don't, but I mean, in them days they did.

110
00:05:25,591 --> 00:05:36,877
[SPEAKER_00]: You'd jump in the car and ride up gravel roads, you know, half an hour, an hour, and played ball and came back, maybe made yourself a $5 bill, and that was your spending money.

111
00:05:36,897 --> 00:05:38,598
[SPEAKER_00]: You didn't get no spending money out of your paycheck.

112
00:05:40,218 --> 00:05:48,663
[SPEAKER_00]: And then later on, we played exhibition games against Philadelphia, Ty Cobb, when Ty Cobb was at Philadelphia in 1927, 28, in that area.

113
00:05:48,743 --> 00:05:53,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, County Mack Motors team down here, my old manager, Jimmy Dykes.

114
00:05:53,636 --> 00:05:54,877
[SPEAKER_00]: play an exhibition game right here.

115
00:05:55,777 --> 00:05:58,519
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'd seen Babe Ruth previous to that, where Babe Ruth was a Yankees.

116
00:05:58,539 --> 00:06:00,501
[SPEAKER_00]: He played exhibition games after World Series.

117
00:06:01,761 --> 00:06:03,162
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was my desire.

118
00:06:04,023 --> 00:06:06,364
[SPEAKER_00]: And I had pretty good luck stroking the ball.

119
00:06:06,424 --> 00:06:07,705
[SPEAKER_00]: I just hit the ball pretty good, you know.

120
00:06:07,725 --> 00:06:08,526
[SPEAKER_00]: I played the outfield.

121
00:06:09,607 --> 00:06:10,507
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't want to play the infield.

122
00:06:10,527 --> 00:06:13,329
[SPEAKER_00]: There was too many bad hops in the infield on these fields over here, you know.

123
00:06:14,610 --> 00:06:15,530
[SPEAKER_00]: So I was an outfielder.

124
00:06:15,570 --> 00:06:16,771
[SPEAKER_00]: And I hit the ball real good.

125
00:06:18,192 --> 00:06:22,235
[SPEAKER_00]: And somebody in Indianapolis got nosy about it, or found out about it.

126
00:06:22,962 --> 00:06:24,043
[SPEAKER_00]: come down here and look me up.

127
00:06:24,063 --> 00:06:28,506
[SPEAKER_00]: But the year previous to that, we had a man over here, he was like a scout, more or less, from Mississauga.

128
00:06:29,226 --> 00:06:34,890
[SPEAKER_00]: He signed me up to a contract for the St. Louis Cards to send me to Danville in the 3I League.

129
00:06:35,650 --> 00:06:37,952
[SPEAKER_00]: That was in 27, I believe it was.

130
00:06:37,992 --> 00:06:44,376
[SPEAKER_00]: No, in 28, because I got married in 28, and I was supposed to go in 29, spring training.

131
00:06:45,456 --> 00:06:50,860
[SPEAKER_00]: And I didn't, my wife didn't want me to go because the baby was coming up, and she said, I'd rather have you around, you know.

132
00:06:51,399 --> 00:06:54,580
[SPEAKER_00]: So I did a report spring training in 1929 to Danville.

133
00:06:55,461 --> 00:06:59,443
[SPEAKER_00]: So June 1921, baby was born.

134
00:07:00,483 --> 00:07:07,426
[SPEAKER_00]: And this man come here from Indianapolis, an Indianapolis scout, to watch me play against Donnie Bush's Pittsburgh Pirates.

135
00:07:08,206 --> 00:07:10,447
[SPEAKER_00]: And Donnie Bush comes in, and he says, Ali Bama?

136
00:07:10,467 --> 00:07:11,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Who's Ali Bama?

137
00:07:11,128 --> 00:07:11,848
[SPEAKER_00]: He's in uniform.

138
00:07:11,888 --> 00:07:12,688
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, we can't play him.

139
00:07:13,509 --> 00:07:17,470
[SPEAKER_00]: See, I was turned into Judge Landis, and I was on the blacklist.

140
00:07:17,630 --> 00:07:21,312
[SPEAKER_00]: See, I was on the blacklist, because I had to report to the St. Louis Cardinals firing team in Danville.

141
00:07:21,966 --> 00:07:22,786
[SPEAKER_00]: I never gave it a thought.

142
00:07:22,906 --> 00:07:23,727
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't know nothing about it.

143
00:07:23,767 --> 00:07:26,887
[SPEAKER_00]: I just did a report that my wife didn't want me to go, so I didn't go.

144
00:07:28,388 --> 00:07:31,449
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the following day, I said, well, I'm going to go to my wife.

145
00:07:31,469 --> 00:07:32,109
[SPEAKER_00]: I asked my wife.

146
00:07:32,969 --> 00:07:34,750
[SPEAKER_00]: We watched the ball game together sitting in the grandstand.

147
00:07:34,770 --> 00:07:35,470
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where he found me.

148
00:07:35,490 --> 00:07:37,530
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead of playing, I was sitting in the grandstand watching the game.

149
00:07:39,191 --> 00:07:41,811
[SPEAKER_00]: And after the game, we drove to my home.

150
00:07:41,832 --> 00:07:43,812
[SPEAKER_00]: And we asked my wife.

151
00:07:43,952 --> 00:07:45,432
[SPEAKER_00]: And my wife, she consented.

152
00:07:45,873 --> 00:07:46,593
[SPEAKER_00]: She says, OK.

153
00:07:47,473 --> 00:07:49,774
[SPEAKER_00]: So the following day, 6 o'clock in the morning, I take off.

154
00:07:49,794 --> 00:07:50,754
[SPEAKER_00]: We go to Indianapolis.

155
00:07:51,922 --> 00:07:57,924
[SPEAKER_00]: And I tried out over there, and they signed me to a contract over there, but I had to get my release from Danville.

156
00:07:58,864 --> 00:07:59,984
[SPEAKER_00]: What they paid for it, I don't know.

157
00:08:00,004 --> 00:08:00,944
[SPEAKER_00]: They must have paid something.

158
00:08:01,025 --> 00:08:04,025
[SPEAKER_00]: If I was good enough to start in AA Ball, they must have given some money.

159
00:08:04,065 --> 00:08:04,646
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know how much.

160
00:08:04,686 --> 00:08:05,446
[SPEAKER_00]: It was none of my business.

161
00:08:05,466 --> 00:08:06,626
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't get any of it myself.

162
00:08:08,186 --> 00:08:11,127
[SPEAKER_00]: But they signed me to a contract, so I stayed with them.

163
00:08:12,528 --> 00:08:13,248
[SPEAKER_00]: What year was this?

164
00:08:13,288 --> 00:08:13,508
[SPEAKER_00]: 1929.

165
00:08:13,528 --> 00:08:15,028
[SPEAKER_01]: 1929, you signed with Indianapolis.

166
00:08:15,068 --> 00:08:15,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Indianapolis Indians.

167
00:08:15,969 --> 00:08:17,009
[SPEAKER_01]: First professional contract.

168
00:08:17,029 --> 00:08:17,769
[SPEAKER_00]: First professional.

169
00:08:17,789 --> 00:08:20,490
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the first contract I signed was with Danville, but I didn't report.

170
00:08:21,610 --> 00:08:23,811
[SPEAKER_00]: but I was still tied down to the professional ball.

171
00:08:25,132 --> 00:08:28,614
[SPEAKER_00]: So I finished 29 with the Indianapolis as an outfielder.

172
00:08:29,475 --> 00:08:35,979
[SPEAKER_00]: And 30, somebody got hurt in Quincy, Illinois, their farm team in May, I guess, or something like that, about a month after the season started.

173
00:08:37,440 --> 00:08:43,884
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was sent down there, and they made an infielder out of me on there, because the second baseman, a shortstop, got hurt.

174
00:08:43,924 --> 00:08:46,966
[SPEAKER_00]: And the second baseman went to play short, and they made the second baseman out of me.

175
00:08:47,926 --> 00:08:48,887
[SPEAKER_00]: And I had some pretty good years.

176
00:08:49,307 --> 00:08:50,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Benson Greenberg was playing with

177
00:08:51,310 --> 00:08:52,030
[SPEAKER_00]: with Evansville.

178
00:08:53,370 --> 00:08:57,351
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Walker brothers were playing with Evansville, Gerald Walker and Hubby Walker.

179
00:08:58,651 --> 00:09:01,592
[SPEAKER_00]: And Tommy Bridges was doing the pitching for them then.

180
00:09:01,612 --> 00:09:04,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, it was all coming up to, you know, coming through.

181
00:09:04,732 --> 00:09:07,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Class B ball, that was, the Three Eyed League was class B ball then.

182
00:09:07,633 --> 00:09:09,453
[SPEAKER_00]: See, now they don't have those kind of leagues anymore.

183
00:09:10,053 --> 00:09:11,834
[SPEAKER_00]: You used to have them all the way down to D ball.

184
00:09:12,614 --> 00:09:14,454
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where you learned your fundamentals from there.

185
00:09:14,474 --> 00:09:16,554
[SPEAKER_00]: And if it was good enough, you moved up every year, see.

186
00:09:17,055 --> 00:09:18,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Now they don't have it.

187
00:09:18,215 --> 00:09:19,695
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess they go out of college now.

188
00:09:20,692 --> 00:09:26,637
[SPEAKER_00]: But them days, now if you don't go to college, I don't think there's much of a chance for you to get into professional baseball, unless you're an exception.

189
00:09:27,538 --> 00:09:29,619
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there are more minors for you to come up.

190
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:33,282
[SPEAKER_00]: So that was 1930.

191
00:09:33,543 --> 00:09:35,484
[SPEAKER_00]: And I stayed there in 30 and 31.

192
00:09:35,524 --> 00:09:36,825
[SPEAKER_00]: I had a good year in 32.

193
00:09:36,845 --> 00:09:40,488
