577
00:28:35,484 --> 00:28:38,805
[SPEAKER_00]: But I always made it, managed to make it go.

578
00:28:40,089 --> 00:28:43,250
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't have to get into no bread lines like a lot of guys did, you know, working in the shop.

579
00:28:43,290 --> 00:28:45,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have a favorite pitcher to hit against in the big ten?

580
00:28:45,970 --> 00:28:47,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Whoo!

581
00:28:47,350 --> 00:28:50,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Not hardly a favorite, but I liked to be bad against left-handers.

582
00:28:50,211 --> 00:28:51,711
[SPEAKER_00]: I liked to be bad against left-handers.

583
00:28:52,692 --> 00:28:56,332
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was the first guy that Robert Feller ever faced in 1936 in St. Louis.

584
00:28:56,412 --> 00:28:58,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a good game?

585
00:28:58,493 --> 00:28:59,233
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no, no, no.

586
00:28:59,533 --> 00:29:03,854
[SPEAKER_00]: When he first come from Des Moines or somebody had him, that's when they had that little controversy, who owns him?

587
00:29:04,414 --> 00:29:06,914
[SPEAKER_00]: And I guess Cleveland had to pay so much money to get him.

588
00:29:07,014 --> 00:29:07,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I was lost then.

589
00:29:07,955 --> 00:29:08,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I know.

590
00:29:10,369 --> 00:29:15,834
[SPEAKER_00]: I forget, well he had a man on first base and second base, and he looked the man up.

591
00:29:15,854 --> 00:29:17,455
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, there was five runs behind or something like that.

592
00:29:17,475 --> 00:29:19,237
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a good time to, you know, break a rookie in, see.

593
00:29:19,857 --> 00:29:24,361
[SPEAKER_00]: So they put him in, put Bob in, and the guy they took out was Pat Malone.

594
00:29:24,381 --> 00:29:26,443
[SPEAKER_00]: He was with you guys for a while.

595
00:29:26,483 --> 00:29:27,824
[SPEAKER_00]: 1936 I guess it was, see.

596
00:29:28,305 --> 00:29:29,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Well he was with us for a while.

597
00:29:30,026 --> 00:29:33,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Well Pat, they took Pat out, and they put Rapid Robert in, see.

598
00:29:34,933 --> 00:29:37,694
[SPEAKER_00]: And he just looked at the base on the back, you know, at second base and fired.

599
00:29:37,734 --> 00:29:39,876
[SPEAKER_00]: And he missed the catcher, missed me, hit the grandstand.

600
00:29:40,276 --> 00:29:41,516
[SPEAKER_00]: And the grandstand just shook.

601
00:29:42,117 --> 00:29:43,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Man, to that guy's throat.

602
00:29:43,757 --> 00:29:44,898
[SPEAKER_00]: Next boy does the same thing.

603
00:29:44,958 --> 00:29:46,519
[SPEAKER_00]: I managed to get a base on balls off of him.

604
00:29:47,819 --> 00:29:48,260
[SPEAKER_00]: But then...

605
00:29:48,320 --> 00:29:52,222
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know whether to sway, duck, or what to do.

606
00:29:53,142 --> 00:29:57,664
[SPEAKER_00]: But the first time he started against us, I'll never forget, that was in Cleveland, maybe a month or two later.

607
00:29:58,585 --> 00:30:01,066
[SPEAKER_00]: And he struck out 16 of us, I guess.

608
00:30:01,106 --> 00:30:02,587
[SPEAKER_00]: He got everybody but Harlan Clift.

609
00:30:03,687 --> 00:30:07,292
[SPEAKER_00]: And Harlan Clift made some kind of remarks in the newspapers, you know, about the sports rather.

610
00:30:07,312 --> 00:30:09,235
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't get everybody, but he didn't get three.

611
00:30:09,676 --> 00:30:12,740
[SPEAKER_00]: So next time he started again, he says he got Harlan Clift three different times.

612
00:30:13,220 --> 00:30:14,342
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't really get that.

613
00:30:14,382 --> 00:30:15,564
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't say much after that.

614
00:30:15,664 --> 00:30:16,605
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he didn't say much.

615
00:30:16,966 --> 00:30:17,767
[SPEAKER_00]: But he was terrific.

616
00:30:18,595 --> 00:30:21,137
[SPEAKER_00]: I know when I went out in 1939, we was in the White Sox.

617
00:30:21,517 --> 00:30:22,858
[SPEAKER_00]: A guy could really hit Robert.

618
00:30:23,038 --> 00:30:27,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Robert, I read stories where Robert was himself in the Rip Radcliffe because he slapped at the ball.

619
00:30:27,480 --> 00:30:28,361
[SPEAKER_00]: He just slapped at the ball.

620
00:30:28,381 --> 00:30:29,722
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't swing, he slapped.

621
00:30:29,962 --> 00:30:31,923
[SPEAKER_00]: The harder you swung, the harder it was to hit him, you know.

622
00:30:32,443 --> 00:30:33,544
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd just slap at that ball.

623
00:30:33,584 --> 00:30:34,605
[SPEAKER_00]: See, he hit him pretty good.

624
00:30:35,845 --> 00:30:40,108
[SPEAKER_00]: But Dykstra would say, don't swing at his curveball, you know.

625
00:30:40,368 --> 00:30:43,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, his curveball was as fast as his fastball, and it dropped, you know.

626
00:30:44,719 --> 00:30:47,600
[SPEAKER_00]: So when he come back, I thought it was his fastball.

627
00:30:47,720 --> 00:30:48,560
[SPEAKER_00]: And it was, you know.

628
00:30:48,921 --> 00:30:50,861
[SPEAKER_00]: It got so far and all of a sudden it left.

629
00:30:51,201 --> 00:30:53,442
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't a slow roundhouse like some guys do, you know.

630
00:30:53,842 --> 00:30:56,123
[SPEAKER_00]: This was like a fastball and just down the side.

631
00:30:57,344 --> 00:30:58,024
[SPEAKER_00]: But he was terrific.

632
00:30:58,064 --> 00:30:59,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Who was your best pitcher at the Browns?

633
00:31:00,105 --> 00:31:00,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Best pitcher?

634
00:31:01,205 --> 00:31:03,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Bobo Newsom was our best pitcher.

635
00:31:03,086 --> 00:31:04,566
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you remember Bobo?

636
00:31:04,586 --> 00:31:05,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, Bobo.

637
00:31:05,987 --> 00:31:07,528
[SPEAKER_00]: All kinds of stories about him.

638
00:31:07,568 --> 00:31:10,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Bobo, he's a dandy.

639
00:31:10,809 --> 00:31:14,190
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll tell you a story about Hemsley again.

640
00:31:15,499 --> 00:31:26,442
[SPEAKER_00]: And, well, Bobo, I'll tell you this one about, in Detroit, we could get beer, go through the restaurant, if you, you know, Hardincliffe and I always signed, one day we'd have a bottle of beer a piece, you know, and we'd sign it.

641
00:31:26,462 --> 00:31:29,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's like a check, you know, so we wouldn't pay for it.

642
00:31:30,243 --> 00:31:31,923
[SPEAKER_00]: The bellhop would bring it up, we'd sign it.

643
00:31:32,903 --> 00:31:43,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, the ball club wasn't supposed to pick it up, but they would pick it up, because in Detroit, at the Book Cadillac, it was going through the kitchen as part of the meal, see.

644
00:31:44,117 --> 00:31:47,478
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, when you had your clothes clean, so you hadn't washed or anything like that, there was all extras.

645
00:31:47,878 --> 00:31:48,679
[SPEAKER_00]: See, all extras.

646
00:31:48,699 --> 00:31:53,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you took care, when you was checking out of the hotel, you'd go to the desk and ask for your extras.

647
00:31:53,981 --> 00:31:58,543
[SPEAKER_00]: So we had two bottles one day, two bottles the next day, you know, and there was no extras, see.

648
00:31:59,743 --> 00:32:10,167
[SPEAKER_00]: And we'd go to bed at night, right above us, there was, you know, about 11 o'clock, there was noise going on, you know, we looked up and there was Hemsley and Buck and those guys having a lot of fun up there, see.

649
00:32:10,892 --> 00:32:12,012
[SPEAKER_00]: And they were bringing it up there.

650
00:32:12,032 --> 00:32:13,213
[SPEAKER_00]: There was about a half a dozen guys.

651
00:32:13,233 --> 00:32:14,553
[SPEAKER_00]: They were bringing it up there by the case.

652
00:32:15,353 --> 00:32:15,573
[SPEAKER_00]: See?

653
00:32:16,233 --> 00:32:17,274
[SPEAKER_00]: And they were signing for it.

654
00:32:18,554 --> 00:32:20,315
[SPEAKER_00]: So at that time, it was $12, two bits a bottle.

655
00:32:20,335 --> 00:32:22,135
[SPEAKER_00]: That's about $12 a case, isn't it?

656
00:32:22,195 --> 00:32:22,515
[SPEAKER_00]: $6 a case.

657
00:32:23,135 --> 00:32:23,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, two bits a bottle.

658
00:32:24,656 --> 00:32:25,336
[SPEAKER_00]: $6 a case.

659
00:32:25,596 --> 00:32:26,776
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what they were, two bits a bottle.

660
00:32:28,457 --> 00:32:30,777
[SPEAKER_00]: And we always stay down to our two.

661
00:32:30,958 --> 00:32:32,618
[SPEAKER_00]: But these guys get down to a case.

662
00:32:33,237 --> 00:32:47,343
[SPEAKER_00]: So one day, Hornsby had a meeting, and he says, Hemsley and Mr. Newsome, he says, we don't mind paying for your food, he says, but if you're gonna bring guests to the hotel and feed them, expect the ball club to pay for them, he says, I don't think that's right.

663
00:32:47,583 --> 00:32:51,145
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, you guys, well, he was allowed the four and a half or five bucks a day then, see?

664
00:32:51,765 --> 00:32:54,146
[SPEAKER_00]: And these guys were eating eight and nine dollars a day, see?

665
00:32:54,366 --> 00:32:55,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Six dollars was beer.

666
00:32:58,148 --> 00:33:01,850
[SPEAKER_00]: So Hornsby never did find out, and he just told them to cut it out, you know?

667
00:33:03,022 --> 00:33:04,803
[SPEAKER_00]: If you bring a friend in, you pay for his meal.

668
00:33:04,824 --> 00:33:06,004
[SPEAKER_00]: The club shouldn't pay for his meal.

669
00:33:06,205 --> 00:33:07,686
[SPEAKER_00]: And there weren't meals, there was just beer.

670
00:33:08,667 --> 00:33:10,548
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd sign one day, the other guy signed the other day.

671
00:33:10,568 --> 00:33:13,330
[SPEAKER_00]: We were here, we were just getting a bottle of beer, and these guys were getting by the case, see.

672
00:33:15,052 --> 00:33:16,793
[SPEAKER_01]: You call him Mr. Hensley, Mr.?

673
00:33:16,973 --> 00:33:18,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Buck, or whatever it was.

674
00:33:18,415 --> 00:33:20,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Buck?

675
00:33:20,977 --> 00:33:22,898
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, Buck, or Al Bobo.

676
00:33:23,619 --> 00:33:25,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, he had, he had some good days.

677
00:33:25,760 --> 00:33:31,265
[SPEAKER_00]: I know he had, always behind a rule one time, if you had two stripes on a batter,

678
00:33:32,063 --> 00:33:35,604
[SPEAKER_00]: And he'd hit a two-nothing pitch and get a base hit.

679
00:33:35,984 --> 00:33:37,085
[SPEAKER_00]: Cost you 25 bucks.

680
00:33:37,105 --> 00:33:39,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, 25 bucks didn't mean a lot of money to you.

681
00:33:40,206 --> 00:33:41,986
[SPEAKER_00]: So Buck Newsome had two strikes on the guy.

682
00:33:42,907 --> 00:33:45,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Sort of high outside pitch, and this guy reaches out for it.

683
00:33:45,508 --> 00:33:48,669
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't a good pitch, but he reached out and slapped it into right field for a base hit.

684
00:33:48,969 --> 00:33:50,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Cost Buck 25 bucks.

685
00:33:50,729 --> 00:33:56,371
[SPEAKER_00]: So every time Buck got ahead of a man, two strikes and no balls, he'd hit the gravestone where there was nobody on base.

686
00:33:56,951 --> 00:33:58,232
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd make sure he wasn't going to hit it.

687
00:33:59,697 --> 00:34:04,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, you can pitch a ball, a bad ball, a guy still can reach out and reach, hit it, you know.

688
00:34:04,521 --> 00:34:06,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Be lucky enough to get a base hit and cost you 25 bucks.

689
00:34:08,524 --> 00:34:09,504
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's how lucky he was.

690
00:34:09,544 --> 00:34:10,585
[SPEAKER_00]: He was strict as a dickens.

691
00:34:10,805 --> 00:34:12,627
[SPEAKER_01]: How about, was Blayholder still with you?

692
00:34:12,667 --> 00:34:13,788
[SPEAKER_00]: George was with us, George.

693
00:34:13,808 --> 00:34:15,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Blayholder was one of us.

694
00:34:15,929 --> 00:34:16,970
[SPEAKER_00]: One of our pitchers, yeah.

695
00:34:17,370 --> 00:34:19,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, slow going, slow, he's from the South.

696
00:34:19,572 --> 00:34:20,933
[SPEAKER_00]: What was he like?

697
00:34:20,953 --> 00:34:22,934
[SPEAKER_00]: He, George, oh, he was happy-go-lucky.

698
00:34:22,954 --> 00:34:24,836
[SPEAKER_00]: He just, he was like a loner.

699
00:34:25,096 --> 00:34:27,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Then we had another guy, I can't think of his name, but he was heavy drinker.

700
00:34:28,822 --> 00:34:32,143
[SPEAKER_00]: And Harvey would always say, if you want a drink, he says, drink in your room.

701
00:34:32,163 --> 00:34:34,064
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, don't go out where the public can see you, you know.

702
00:34:34,664 --> 00:34:36,725
[SPEAKER_00]: And he says, you want to go drink because you had a bad day?

703
00:34:36,745 --> 00:34:38,686
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, go out by yourself because nobody knows you.

704
00:34:39,026 --> 00:34:40,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't go out with your friend you think is your friend.

705
00:34:40,967 --> 00:34:46,469
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he's the first, he's the first guy calls the front office the following day, says he was out with you, he was out with you.

706
00:34:47,110 --> 00:34:48,830
[SPEAKER_00]: And he does the complaining, you know.

707
00:34:49,551 --> 00:34:50,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Which has been since.

708
00:34:50,491 --> 00:34:52,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Was there much drinking in your time?

709
00:34:52,112 --> 00:34:52,972
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it wasn't, no.

710
00:34:53,012 --> 00:34:55,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Not excessive, but I mean, some guys would.

711
00:34:55,673 --> 00:34:57,854
[SPEAKER_00]: You had one or two on a club with Bobby Overduke.

712
00:34:58,193 --> 00:34:59,795
[SPEAKER_00]: because there's nothing wrong with having a drink.

713
00:34:59,835 --> 00:35:02,858
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean a beer, especially because I didn't drink the booze, I just drank beer myself.

714
00:35:03,339 --> 00:35:04,900
[SPEAKER_00]: But some of these guys would drink that stuff.

715
00:35:05,040 --> 00:35:08,825
[SPEAKER_00]: They had a bad day, probably like what they say, when it drowns your sorrows, I guess, or something like that.

716
00:35:08,885 --> 00:35:13,449
[SPEAKER_00]: And, no, that wasn't... You never went to the ballpark that way any other time?

717
00:35:13,470 --> 00:35:14,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, no, no.

718
00:35:14,050 --> 00:35:15,472
[SPEAKER_00]: I never saw any of them come out that way.

719
00:35:15,532 --> 00:35:16,673
[SPEAKER_00]: No, not to the ballpark, no.

720
00:35:17,897 --> 00:35:23,439
[SPEAKER_00]: But you go to work during 29, 30, and 31, I was playing, that was prohibition, you know.

721
00:35:23,459 --> 00:35:24,400
[SPEAKER_00]: There was no drinking at all.

722
00:35:24,420 --> 00:35:26,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, you get a bottle of homebrew somewhere.

723
00:35:26,140 --> 00:35:28,521
[SPEAKER_00]: You always find out you get a bottle of homebrew, you know.

724
00:35:28,541 --> 00:35:32,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, we used to go, when I was at Quincy in the Three-Eyed League, my brother was over there.

725
00:35:32,903 --> 00:35:41,006
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd come pay me a visit, and we used to go across into, this guy, he wrote a story about the guy on a fence, what was his name?

726
00:35:41,026 --> 00:35:44,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Tom Sawyer, yeah, what was the name of that little town?

727
00:35:45,103 --> 00:35:48,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Cow, that's right, right across the river, Hannibal, Missouri, right across the river from Quincy.

728
00:35:49,124 --> 00:35:52,904
[SPEAKER_00]: We used to go to Hannibal and get a couple bottles of beer after it was, after the ball game.

729
00:35:53,044 --> 00:35:54,385
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd ride a ferry across, you know.

730
00:35:56,545 --> 00:35:58,885
[SPEAKER_00]: But, uh, I learned that.

731
00:35:58,945 --> 00:36:00,206
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't do too much drinking.

732
00:36:00,226 --> 00:36:00,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Jack Burns?

733
00:36:01,306 --> 00:36:02,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Jack Burns was the first baseman, yes.

734
00:36:03,006 --> 00:36:04,586
[SPEAKER_00]: He remembered that name better than I do, guys.

735
00:36:04,927 --> 00:36:06,167
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm trying to say, he was from Boston.

736
00:36:07,127 --> 00:36:07,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Jack, yeah.

737
00:36:07,727 --> 00:36:08,387
[SPEAKER_00]: What about him?

738
00:36:09,067 --> 00:36:10,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he was, well, he was a quiet guy.

739
00:36:10,828 --> 00:36:13,248
[SPEAKER_00]: He had a boy, but I had my girl at that time.

740
00:36:14,112 --> 00:36:16,073
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was a good first baseman.

741
00:36:16,093 --> 00:36:17,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Jack was a good hustler too.

742
00:36:17,313 --> 00:36:19,594
[SPEAKER_00]: He was all right.

743
00:36:20,794 --> 00:36:23,935
[SPEAKER_00]: And Hemsley again now in St. Louis, he dressed up.

744
00:36:23,955 --> 00:36:27,697
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason they called him hobo though, the way I understand, he had a brand new suit of clothes on.

745
00:36:27,737 --> 00:36:29,337
[SPEAKER_00]: By the time he'd come back, he looked like a bum.

746
00:36:29,357 --> 00:36:30,578
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know what he'd do with himself.

747
00:36:31,938 --> 00:36:33,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Hobo, that's why they called him hobo.

748
00:36:33,719 --> 00:36:34,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you hear?

749
00:36:34,139 --> 00:36:35,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh yeah, that's hobo.

750
00:36:35,999 --> 00:36:37,560
[SPEAKER_00]: And one time he had a

751
00:36:38,516 --> 00:36:44,819
[SPEAKER_00]: A brand new car, and I don't know, a post or something got in his way, and his daughter told my daughter a telephone post got in my daddy's way.

752
00:36:45,259 --> 00:36:46,239
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why he hit the post.

753
00:36:46,579 --> 00:36:47,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody got in his way.

754
00:36:48,780 --> 00:36:51,441
[SPEAKER_00]: But yeah, that's how they call him Homer.

755
00:36:51,481 --> 00:36:52,642
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd be dressed up.

756
00:36:53,702 --> 00:36:55,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Next day, the suit wouldn't look the same.

757
00:36:56,583 --> 00:36:58,784
[SPEAKER_01]: Another name I was always fascinated by was Storty.

758
00:36:59,824 --> 00:37:00,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Storty.

759
00:37:00,264 --> 00:37:01,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Glenn Storty.

760
00:37:01,185 --> 00:37:02,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Glenn, yeah, yeah.

761
00:37:02,245 --> 00:37:04,046
[SPEAKER_01]: He was there before me, I think.

762
00:37:04,086 --> 00:37:05,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, Storty was before me, yeah.

763
00:37:07,372 --> 00:37:10,514
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess Levy and Starry were... Yeah, they were there about the time, yeah.

764
00:37:11,474 --> 00:37:12,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Levy played football, didn't he?

765
00:37:13,815 --> 00:37:17,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I can't recall him playing football, but he went to school somewheres, I don't know.

766
00:37:17,577 --> 00:37:19,539
